VALUES (
  '2026/11/10/the-relief-rally-is-a-test-of-recency-bias-not-proof-the-risk-has-passed',
  'behaviour',
  'article',
  'The Relief Rally Is a Test of Recency Bias, Not Proof the Risk Has Passed',
  'A 2.3% S&P 500 rebound and a 5.85% Brent decline offer a textbook temptation to extrapolate one day of relief into a durable change in risk.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Monday produced the strongest US equity session since April,</strong><span> according to AP, as falling oil prices eased inflation concerns.</span></li>
<li><strong>The S&amp;P 500 gained 2.3%,</strong><span> the Dow 2.2%, the Nasdaq 2.7% and small-cap Russell 2000 3.1%.</span></li>
<li><strong>The trigger was a repricing of energy and geopolitical risk,</strong><span> not confirmation of a lasting peace agreement.</span></li>
<li><strong>Recency bias encourages clients to mistake a single powerful session for a new trend.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Advisors can turn the rebound into a conversation about predetermined rebalancing rules.</strong></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>Monday produced the strongest US equity session since April, according to AP, as falling oil prices eased inflation concerns. The S&amp;P 500 gained 2.3%, the Dow 2.2%, the Nasdaq 2.7% and small-cap Russell 2000 3.1%. The important distinction for an advisor is between the reported observation and the inference investors are tempted to draw from it.</p><h2>Why Relief Feels Like Certainty</h2><p>The trigger was a repricing of energy and geopolitical risk, not confirmation of a lasting peace agreement. The S&amp;P 500 gained 2.3%, the Dow 2.2%, the Nasdaq 2.7% and small-cap Russell 2000 3.1%. This matters because the same headline can have different consequences for a long-horizon investor, a household funding near-term expenses and a client with a concentrated position. The correct response depends on cash-flow needs, valuation and risk capacity, not simply on the direction of the latest print.</p><p>For Canadian advisors, the practical question is which assumption in the client plan has actually changed. A temporary change in sentiment may alter quoted prices without altering future liabilities. Conversely, a regulatory proposal, an employment deterioration or a persistent supply disruption may change the expected cash flows that underpin the plan. The distinction is central to a disciplined review.</p><h2>The Difference Between New Information and a New Narrative</h2><p>Recency bias encourages clients to mistake a single powerful session for a new trend. The trigger was a repricing of energy and geopolitical risk, not confirmation of a lasting peace agreement. This matters because the same headline can have different consequences for a long-horizon investor, a household funding near-term expenses and a client with a concentrated position. The correct response depends on cash-flow needs, valuation and risk capacity, not simply on the direction of the latest print.</p><p>For Canadian advisors, the practical question is which assumption in the client plan has actually changed. A temporary change in sentiment may alter quoted prices without altering future liabilities. Conversely, a regulatory proposal, an employment deterioration or a persistent supply disruption may change the expected cash flows that underpin the plan. The distinction is central to a disciplined review.</p><div class="hdq-chart"><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;"><div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;">BEHAVIOUR | VERIFIED COMPARISON</div><div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;"><script>(function(){var _cs=document.currentScript;var NS="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg";function el(t,a,v){var e=document.createElementNS(NS,t);Object.keys(a).forEach(function(k){e.setAttribute(k,a[k]);});if(v!==undefined)e.textContent=v;return e;}var svg=el("svg",{viewBox:"0 0 680 300",width:"100%",role:"img","aria-label":"behaviour verified comparison"});var labels=["S&P 500", "Dow", "Nasdaq", "Russell 2000"],values=[2.3, 2.2, 2.7, 3.1],max=3.1;svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:205,y1:15,x2:205,y2:265,stroke:"#dddddd"}));values.forEach(function(v,i){var y=28+i*(225/values.length);var w=Math.abs(v)/max*375;svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:8,y:y+15,"font-size":12,fill:"#444444"},labels[i]));svg.appendChild(el("rect",{x:205,y:y,width:w,height:25,fill:v<0?"#b65a52":"#526a80",rx:2}));svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:210+w,y:y+17,"font-size":12,fill:"#222222"},String(v)));});_cs.parentNode.appendChild(svg);})();</script></div><div style="font-size:10px;color:#888;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Associated Press, US market close, Nov 9 2026. Values reflect source units as labelled. | hdq.ca</div></div></div><p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;">Chart note: The chart compares reported figures; it is not a forecast.</p><h2>An Advisor Response That Does Not Depend on Tuesday</h2><p>Advisors can turn the rebound into a conversation about predetermined rebalancing rules. Recency bias encourages clients to mistake a single powerful session for a new trend. This matters because the same headline can have different consequences for a long-horizon investor, a household funding near-term expenses and a client with a concentrated position. The correct response depends on cash-flow needs, valuation and risk capacity, not simply on the direction of the latest print.</p><p>For Canadian advisors, the practical question is which assumption in the client plan has actually changed. A temporary change in sentiment may alter quoted prices without altering future liabilities. Conversely, a regulatory proposal, an employment deterioration or a persistent supply disruption may change the expected cash flows that underpin the plan. The distinction is central to a disciplined review.</p><p>Advisors can turn the rebound into a conversation about predetermined rebalancing rules. A documented decision rule, scenario analysis and a scheduled follow-up can be more useful than a confident prediction. Figures and policy status are stated as of the 6:48 a.m. Toronto research cutoff on November 10, 2026.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div><p>Clients may be torn between relief at the latest move and concern that the underlying uncertainty has not disappeared. Those who recently changed allocations may be especially sensitive to the next headline.</p></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div><p>The latest numbers deserve attention, but they do not by themselves require a portfolio change. We can examine what has changed, what remains uncertain and whether your existing plan still fits.</p></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div><p>Clients with concentrated positions, upcoming withdrawals, refinancing needs or a short investment horizon merit a more specific discussion.</p></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div><ul><li>Review concentration and liquidity needs.</li><li>Compare current exposures with the written investment policy.</li><li>Check dates and legislative status before implementing tax changes.</li><li>Document the rationale for any recommended adjustment.</li></ul></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div><div class="email-template" id="respond-email"><strong>Subject: A useful perspective on behaviour developments</strong><br><br>Hi [Client Name],<br><br>I wanted to share a quick update on behaviour. Monday produced the strongest US equity session since April, according to AP, as falling oil prices eased inflation concerns. This is a useful moment to review your objectives and current positioning, rather than react to a single session. Please let me know if you would like to schedule a brief review.<br><br>Best regards,<br>[Your Name]<br><br><em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em></div><button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button></div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div><p><strong>Clients with concentrated exposures:</strong> households whose decisions are most sensitive to the latest behaviour developments.</p><p><strong>Investors approaching a financial decision:</strong> people weighing a portfolio change, financing choice or liquidity event.</p></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div><p>The latest headlines create an opportunity to separate the immediate market response from what actually changed in the underlying facts.</p></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div><p>Offer a concise, evidence-based review of the client’s exposures, decision deadlines and downside scenarios, without promising a market outcome.</p></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div><ul><li>What prompted you to reconsider your current strategy?</li><li>Which upcoming financial commitment matters most?</li><li>What level of volatility would require you to change your plan?</li></ul></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div><div class="email-template" id="prospect-email"><strong>Subject: A useful perspective on behaviour developments</strong><br><br>Hi [Client Name],<br><br>I noticed the latest developments around behaviour. Monday produced the strongest US equity session since April, according to AP, as falling oil prices eased inflation concerns. Rather than make a decision from one headline, I would be glad to share a short framework for assessing what matters for your goals. Would a 15-minute conversation be useful?<br><br>Best regards,<br>[Your Name]<br><br><em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em></div><button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button></div>',
  '[{"value": "2.3%", "label": "S&P 500 Monday gain"}, {"value": "2.7%", "label": "Nasdaq Monday gain"}, {"value": "3.1%", "label": "Russell 2000 gain"}, {"value": "5.85%", "label": "Brent Monday decline"}]',
