VALUES (
  '2026/11/10/ottawa-proposes-may-15-tax-filing-and-a-15-million-growth-exemption-but-neither-is-law-yet',
  'tax',
  'article',
  'Ottawa Proposes May 15 Tax Filing and a $15 Million Growth Exemption, but Neither Is Law Yet',
  'Budget 2026 would move the personal filing deadline, double foreign reporting thresholds and create a tightly qualified capital gains incentive.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The November 5 federal budget proposes moving the personal income tax filing and payment deadline to May 15 beginning in 2027.</strong></li>
<li><strong>It also proposes doubling the Form T1135 foreign-property reporting threshold.</strong></li>
<li><strong>The Canada Growth Incentive would offer an exemption of up to $15 million on qualifying share gains.</strong></li>
<li><strong>The budget did not change the headline personal or corporate income tax rates or capital gains inclusion rate.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Advisors must distinguish a budget proposal from enacted legislation before changing client calendars or transactions.</strong></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The November 5 federal budget proposes moving the personal income tax filing and payment deadline to May 15 beginning in 2027. It also proposes doubling the Form T1135 foreign-property reporting threshold. The important distinction for an advisor is between the reported observation and the inference investors are tempted to draw from it.</p><h2>A Calendar Proposal Is Not a Filing Extension Yet</h2><p>The Canada Growth Incentive would offer an exemption of up to $15 million on qualifying share gains. It also proposes doubling the Form T1135 foreign-property reporting threshold. This matters because the same headline can have different consequences for a long-horizon investor, a household funding near-term expenses and a client with a concentrated position. The correct response depends on cash-flow needs, valuation and risk capacity, not simply on the direction of the latest print.</p><p>For Canadian advisors, the practical question is which assumption in the client plan has actually changed. A temporary change in sentiment may alter quoted prices without altering future liabilities. Conversely, a regulatory proposal, an employment deterioration or a persistent supply disruption may change the expected cash flows that underpin the plan. The distinction is central to a disciplined review.</p><h2>The Growth Incentive Is Not a Blanket Exemption</h2><p>The budget did not change the headline personal or corporate income tax rates or capital gains inclusion rate. The Canada Growth Incentive would offer an exemption of up to $15 million on qualifying share gains. This matters because the same headline can have different consequences for a long-horizon investor, a household funding near-term expenses and a client with a concentrated position. The correct response depends on cash-flow needs, valuation and risk capacity, not simply on the direction of the latest print.</p><p>For Canadian advisors, the practical question is which assumption in the client plan has actually 