  'Oil Is Pricing a Pause in Iran Strikes While the Hormuz Blockade Remains',
  'Brent settled at $92.57 after Washington signalled restraint, yet the shipping confrontation and diplomatic disagreements were unresolved.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Brent settled down $5.75 at $92.57 on November 9,</strong><span> while WTI lost $3.98 to $86.85.</span></li>
<li><strong>President Trump said he would hold off on new strikes against Iran for now.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Washington said it would maintain its naval blockade in the Strait of Hormuz.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Iranian proposals and US demands remain apart; neither a ceasefire nor shipping normalization was confirmed.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Advisors should separate the oil price response to diplomatic language from measurable changes in shipping access.</strong></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>Brent settled down $5.75 at $92.57 on November 9, while WTI lost $3.98 to $86.85. President Trump said he would hold off on new strikes against Iran for now. The important distinction for an advisor is between the reported observation and the inference investors are tempted to draw from it.</p><h2>The Price of a Pause</h2><p>Washington said it would maintain its naval blockade in the Strait of Hormuz. President Trump said he would hold off on new strikes against Iran for now. This matters because the same headline can have different consequences for a long-horizon investor, a household funding near-term expenses and a client with a concentrated position. The correct response depends on cash-flow needs, valuation and risk capacity, not simply on the direction of the latest print.</p><p>For Canadian advisors, the practical question is which assumption in the client plan has actually changed. A temporary change in sentiment may alter quoted prices without altering future liabilities. Conversely, a regulatory proposal, an employment deterioration or a persistent supply disruption may change the expected cash flows that underpin the plan. The distinction is central to a disciplined review.</p><h2>Why the Blockade Matters More Than the Headline</h2><p>Iranian proposals and US demands remain apart; neither a ceasefire nor shipping normalization was confirmed. Washington said it would maintain its naval blockade in the Strait of Hormuz. This matters because the same headline can have different consequences for a long-horizon investor, a household funding near-term expenses and a client with a concentrated position. The correct response depends on cash-flow needs, valuation and risk capacity, not simply on the direction of the latest print.</p><p>For Canadian advisors, the practical question is which assumption in the client plan has actually changed. A temporary change in sentiment may alter quoted prices without altering future liabilities. Conversely, a regulatory p